\begin_layout Title
Does Trading From an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Equal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Position Lead to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Fair
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Allocation of Resources?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 3, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In economics, the concept of Pareto efficiecy gives a noteworthy criterion
 concerning how goods in an economy should be allocated efficiently.
 However, it does not address any issues about income distribution, nor
 does it give any 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equity
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 implicaiton.
 Instead, general equilibrium theory provides separate characteristics for
 the notion of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fairness.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 An allocation is said to be fair if it is both Pareto optimal and envy-free,
 i.e., no one envies anyone else.
 According to the theory, if all individuals' begin trading from an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

equal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 position, then this will result in a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fair
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allocaiton.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The type of experiment I would like to design is a theory test for this
 specific result.
 The experiments discussed in class thus far have only been concerned with
 the achievement of the predicted competitive equilibrium, along with the
 Pareto efficiency implications, using a few market institutions.
 Consequently, the main focus variable in my experiment will remain the
 specific market institution being implemented, and it will also act as
 the only treatment variable.
 The double-auction, posted-offer, and bilateral bargaining arrangements
 will be the three separate treatments to be tested.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For now, the same economic environment will be controlled throughout the
 experiment.
 It will be a pure-exchange economy with four goods and twenty-four agents,
 so each agent can act as both a buyer and a seller.
 The preferences of the agents, which are derived from the twenty-four different
 possible rankings of the goods, will be private information and randomly
 assigned to control for important nuisances, such as personal tastes and
 attitudes, while all agents will receive equal endowments of all the goods.
 The agents will be instructed to obtain an allocation of goods that most
 closely resembles their given preferences, and each agent will be given
 monetary rewards based on how well their allocations match their preferences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since all agents are endowed with the same number of goods, the theory implies
 that agents will trade to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fair
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 allocation.
 Changing the market institution in each session of the experiment allows
 me to test which arrangement best leads to a fair allocation of goods.
\end_layout

